course: dictionary
lesson: 2512
title: Deception: tricks that fool you
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Deception means making someone believe something that is not true. It can be a trick, a lie, or anything that fools a person on purpose.
explain: «Magic tricks are deception people agree to enjoy.» Everyone knows a magic trick is a trick, and everything is given back at the end, so nobody is really fooled in a harmful way. Context shows you that deception is not always bad: it depends on whether people agree to it.
explain: Scams use deception. A message that says you have won a prize you never entered is built to make you believe something false, so check before you trust it. Villains in stories use deception too, and so do animals: a stick insect looks like a twig to hide from a bird.
fact: Deception is a noun. It is uncountable when you mean the idea, and you can say a deception when you mean one trick or lie.
fact: «Deception» means making someone believe something that is not true.
fact: «Obtained by deception» means got by tricking someone.
fact: Scams use deception, such as fake messages or fake prizes; check before you trust them.
fact: A joke that both people understand is not deception, because nobody is really fooled.
exercise: «Magic tricks are deception people agree to enjoy.» What does deception mean here? | A rule that must always be obeyed | Stealing from a shop | A sad story told at night | *A trick that everyone knows about and agrees to enjoy
exercise: A message says, «You have won a prize you never entered!» What is this? | *An example of deception, a trick to make you believe something false | A kind gift from a friend | A weather forecast
exercise: A stick insect looks exactly like a twig. Which word means a trick or lie that fools someone? | Patience | Kindness | *Deception | Sunlight
test: «Obtained by deception» means the thing was got | by winning a race fairly | by accident on a sunny day | *by tricking someone | by asking politely
test: Which is an example of deception in nature? | A tree losing its leaves in autumn | A bird singing in the morning | A river flowing to the sea | *An opossum that plays dead to fool a predator
exam: Which is NOT deception? | A fake prize message asking for your password | *A joke that both people understand and enjoy | A stick insect that looks like a twig | A villain pretending to be a friend
